faqs:
  - question: "Which AI tools have an official Appwrite plugin?"
    answer: "Claude Code, Codex, ChatGPT, and Cursor. Codex and ChatGPT share one plugin, Appwrite's official ChatGPT plugin. Each plugin bundles the hosted [Appwrite MCP server](/docs/tooling/ai/mcp-servers) and [Appwrite Skills](/docs/tooling/ai/skills) for the CLI and the major SDKs. Other tools connect with the MCP server URL and `npx skills add appwrite/skills`."
  - question: "Do I need an API key to connect my AI assistant to Appwrite Cloud?"
    answer: "No. The hosted MCP server at `https://mcp.appwrite.io/` uses OAuth. Your browser opens, you sign in to your Appwrite account, and you approve access. A self-hosted Appwrite instance uses the [local MCP server](/docs/advanced/self-hosting/mcp) with an API key instead."
  - question: "What access does the Appwrite MCP sign-in give my agent?"
    answer: "By default, **Full access**: the tool can manage your organizations, projects, and their data on your behalf. On the consent screen you can limit **Project access** to specific projects, and **Customize access** lets you make every permission **Read-only** or choose **Read** or **Read + Write** per permission."
  - question: "How do I revoke an AI tool's access to my Appwrite account?"
    answer: "In the Appwrite Console, open the account menu, go to **Account** > **Applications**, and click **Revoke** next to the tool, or **Revoke all** if it signed in more than once. Its tokens stop working, and the tool has to sign in again before it can reach your projects."
  - question: "Do I still need Appwrite Skills if my agent is connected to the MCP server?"
    answer: "Yes. The MCP server lets the agent act on your project and search the docs. Skills shape the code it writes in your repository, for example by telling it to use `TablesDB` instead of the legacy `Databases` class. The Appwrite plugins include both."
  - question: "Why does my AI agent write databases.createDocument instead of tablesDB.createRow?"
    answer: "It is writing from training data that predates TablesDB. Install the Appwrite skill for your SDK with `npx skills add appwrite/skills` (or use an Appwrite plugin), start a new session, and ask again. The skill tells the agent to use `TablesDB` with object parameters for all new code."
---

AI coding assistants write Appwrite code from what they saw in training. That often means the legacy `Databases` API with collections and documents instead of tables and rows, and permissions picked to make an error go away rather than to protect your users' data.

Appwrite Skills fix the outdated code. Permissions stay yours to check, and [prompt 4](#prompt-permissions) shows you how.

If you use Claude Code, Codex, ChatGPT, or Cursor, install the official **Appwrite plugin**. It bundles the hosted **Appwrite MCP server**, which lets the agent act on your project, and **Appwrite Skills**, Markdown files that teach it the current SDKs.

Any other tool gets the same result from one MCP URL and one Skills command. Then the five prompts in step 4 wire Appwrite into your own codebase.

{% only_dark %}
![Diagram: your AI tool loads the Appwrite plugin. Its Skills shape the code in your repository, and its MCP server reaches your Appwrite project only through the OAuth consent you approve](/images/blog/connect-ai-coding-assistant-to-appwrite/dark/plugin-flow.avif)
{% /only_dark %}
{% only_light %}
![Diagram: your AI tool loads the Appwrite plugin. Its Skills shape the code in your repository, and its MCP server reaches your Appwrite project only through the OAuth consent you approve](/images/blog/connect-ai-coding-assistant-to-appwrite/plugin-flow.avif)
{% /only_light %}

**Start where you are:**

- No Appwrite project yet: create one in the [Appwrite Console](/sign-in), then go to [step 1](#install-plugin) if your tool has a plugin or [step 2](#mcp-and-skills) if it doesn't. The project has to exist before you can limit the agent to it.
- Using Claude Code, Codex, or Cursor: start at [step 1](#install-plugin).
- Using ChatGPT: install the plugin in [step 1](#install-plugin), then run [step 4](#first-session) in Codex, which works on the files in your repository.
- Using VS Code, Windsurf, Zed, OpenCode, Antigravity, or another MCP client: go to [step 2](#mcp-and-skills).
- Already connected, but you clicked through the sign-in screen: [step 3](#oauth-access) shows what you granted and how to narrow or revoke it.
- Connected and signed in: go to [step 4](#first-session). If you added MCP by hand, [install Skills](#install-skills) first.
- Finished the session: go to [your first database and row](#first-database).

# Step 1: Install the Appwrite plugin for your tool {% #install-plugin %}

Pick the plugin whenever your tool has one. It installs and updates the MCP server and the Skills together, so you don't keep two installs in sync.

{% tabs %}
{% tabsitem #claude-code title="Claude Code" %}

Install the plugin from Anthropic's official marketplace:

```bash
claude plugin install appwrite@claude-plugins-official
```

Then connect it:

1. In a session that is already open, run `/reload-plugins`.
2. Run `/mcp`, select the Appwrite server, and choose **Authenticate**.
3. On the consent screen that opens, narrow access as in [step 3](#oauth-access), then click **Authorize**.

You'll know it worked when `/mcp` shows the Appwrite server as connected. Don't also run `claude mcp add` for Appwrite: the plugin already includes the server, and adding it again registers it twice.

If Claude Code can't find the plugin, install it from Appwrite's own marketplace as in the [Claude Code docs](/docs/tooling/ai/agents/claude-code#install-plugin). If an older install asks for an API key, open `/plugins`, go to the **Installed** tab, and update **Appwrite**.

{% /tabsitem %}
{% tabsitem #codex title="Codex" %}

Codex uses the same official plugin as ChatGPT.

1. In the Codex CLI, run `/plugins`, switch to the **OpenAI Curated** marketplace, search for `Appwrite`, and select **Install plugin**. In the Codex desktop app, it's under **Plugins** in the sidebar.
2. Sign in to Appwrite when prompted, and narrow access as in [step 3](#oauth-access) before you click **Authorize**.
3. Start a new Codex session so the skills load, and ask it to list your Appwrite projects.

You'll know it worked when it returns them by name.

If `codex plugin list` shows `appwrite@appwrite`, you have an early version without the hosted MCP server. Remove it, then install from OpenAI Curated:

```bash
codex plugin remove appwrite@appwrite
codex plugin marketplace remove appwrite
```

{% /tabsitem %}
{% tabsitem #chatgpt title="ChatGPT" %}

1. In the ChatGPT desktop app, open **Plugins** in the sidebar, search for `Appwrite`, open the **Appwrite** listing, and click **Install plugin**.
2. Follow the connection prompt to sign in to Appwrite, and narrow access as in [step 3](#oauth-access) before you click **Authorize**.
3. Start a new conversation and ask it to list your Appwrite projects.

You'll know it worked when it returns them by name.

ChatGPT is for inspecting your project. Run the coding session in [step 4](#first-session) in Codex, which works on your repository's files with the same plugin. The Codex tab shows the install.

{% /tabsitem %}
{% tabsitem #cursor title="Cursor" %}

Install the [Appwrite plugin](https://cursor.com/marketplace/appwrite) from the Cursor Marketplace. It adds the skills, two deploy commands, and the hosted MCP server under the name `appwrite`.

Cursor opens the Appwrite sign-in when the server first connects. Narrow access as in [step 3](#oauth-access) before you click **Authorize**. You'll know it worked when the `appwrite` MCP server lists its tools instead of a login prompt.

If it doesn't connect, or the sign-in expires later, turn `appwrite` off and on and sign in again. Servers named `appwrite-api` and `appwrite-docs` are the old local setup that needs an API key, so reinstall the plugin. If no Appwrite server is listed, use the one-click link in the [Cursor setup docs](/docs/tooling/ai/agents/cursor#step-3).

{% /tabsitem %}
{% /tabs %}

# Step 2: Connect any other AI coding assistant with MCP and Skills {% #mcp-and-skills %}

Skip this step if you installed a plugin. Every other tool needs two installs: the remote MCP server and the Skills.

## Add the Appwrite MCP server {% #add-mcp %}

The MCP server is one URL, `https://mcp.appwrite.io/`, with OAuth sign-in and no API key. Each tool wraps it in its own config format, so copy the snippet from your tool's guide, not another tool's file.

Use the guide for [VS Code](/docs/tooling/ai/agents/vscode), [Windsurf](/docs/tooling/ai/agents/windsurf), [Zed](/docs/tooling/ai/agents/zed), [OpenCode](/docs/tooling/ai/agents/opencode), or [Antigravity](/docs/tooling/ai/agents/antigravity), which needs `serverUrl` and fails silently on `url`. The [MCP server docs](/docs/tooling/ai/mcp-servers#installation) list the rest.

When the Appwrite consent screen opens, narrow access as in [step 3](#oauth-access) before you click **Authorize**. You'll know it worked when your tool lists `appwrite_get_context` among the server's tools.

## Install Appwrite Skills {% #install-skills %}

Run this from your project's root:

```bash
npx skills add appwrite/skills
```

The installer asks four questions:

- Select only the SDKs this project uses.
- Select every AI tool you work with.
- Choose **project** scope, so a project on a different SDK gets its own set.
- Choose **symlink**, so all your tools share one copy.

You'll know it worked when your project has a `.agents/skills/` folder with your SDK's skill in it, such as `appwrite-typescript`. Start a new agent session so the skills load.

# Step 3: Choose what the Appwrite sign-in grants {% #oauth-access %}

The first time your tool connects, your browser opens an Appwrite consent screen. Its default is **Full access**, which lets the tool manage your organizations, projects, and their data on your behalf. Narrow it before you click **Authorize**:

1. Under **Project access**, which shows **All projects**, click **Change** and choose **Specific projects**.
2. Search for the project you're building, select it, and click **Done**.
3. Do the same under **Organization access** for its organization, so a wrong guess by the agent can't reach anything else.

{% only_dark %}
![Appwrite consent screen with Project access set to one project, Organization access set to its organization, and the Authorize button below](/images/blog/connect-ai-coding-assistant-to-appwrite/dark/consent-project-access.avif)
{% /only_dark %}
{% only_light %}
![Appwrite consent screen with Project access set to one project, Organization access set to its organization, and the Authorize button below](/images/blog/connect-ai-coding-assistant-to-appwrite/consent-project-access.avif)
{% /only_light %}

**Customize access** limits what the tool can do. It has a **Read-only** option and a **Read** or **Read + Write** level for each permission. Leave write access on for the session below, because the agent creates a table. Turn on **Read-only** when you point an agent at a project with real users.

{% only_dark %}
![Appwrite consent screen with Customize access open, showing the Read-only option and a Read or Read + Write choice for each permission](/images/blog/connect-ai-coding-assistant-to-appwrite/dark/consent-customize.avif)
{% /only_dark %}
{% only_light %}
![Appwrite consent screen with Customize access open, showing the Read-only option and a Read or Read + Write choice for each permission](/images/blog/connect-ai-coding-assistant-to-appwrite/consent-customize.avif)
{% /only_light %}

Write access can mean silent changes. The MCP server runs a write only when the model passes `confirm_write: true`, but the model sets that flag itself, so your only check is your tool's approval prompt. For this session, turn off any auto-approve or auto-run mode and approve each write by hand.

Every sign-in appears in the Console under the account menu > **Account** > **Applications**. **Revoke** (or **Revoke all**, for a tool that signed in more than once) ends that tool's access, including every token it holds.

{% only_dark %}
![Applications page in the Appwrite Console account settings, listing an authorized AI tool with a Revoke button](/images/blog/connect-ai-coding-assistant-to-appwrite/dark/account-applications.avif)
{% /only_dark %}
{% only_light %}
![Applications page in the Appwrite Console account settings, listing an authorized AI tool with a Revoke button](/images/blog/connect-ai-coding-assistant-to-appwrite/account-applications.avif)
{% /only_light %}

Revoking is also how you widen access later: revoke, reconnect, and choose the new project on the consent screen.

If your tool runs on a remote machine over SSH, the sign-in's `localhost` callback lands on that machine. [Authenticate from a remote machine](/docs/tooling/ai/mcp-servers#remote) covers the fix for Claude Code.

# Step 4: Wire Appwrite into your app in five prompts {% #first-session %}

Open your app's root folder in your tool. With no app yet, open an empty folder, and prompt 2 clones a starter into it. Send the five prompts in order, one at a time, and replace the parts in angle brackets with a real feature of your app, such as tasks or notes.

The code sample and the CORS check assume a web app. On another platform, the agent uses the SDK from prompt 2, and the permission checks stay the same.

## Prompt 1: Confirm the organization and project {% #prompt-project %}

```text
Use Appwrite to show my workspace context: my organizations, the projects in each, and their IDs. Don't change anything. Tell me which project you'll use for this repository and wait for my confirmation.
```

The agent answers with `appwrite_get_context`. Compare the ID it picks with the **Project ID** on your project's **Settings** page in the Console. Names can repeat across organizations, so match the ID, not the name.

{% only_dark %}
![Project Settings page in the Appwrite Console with the Project ID and API endpoint under API credentials](/images/blog/connect-ai-coding-assistant-to-appwrite/dark/project-settings-id.avif)
{% /only_dark %}
{% only_light %}
![Project Settings page in the Appwrite Console with the Project ID and API endpoint under API credentials](/images/blog/connect-ai-coding-assistant-to-appwrite/project-settings-id.avif)
{% /only_light %}

If the project you want is missing, your sign-in doesn't cover it. Revoke and reconnect as in [step 3](#oauth-access).

## Prompt 2: Wire the Appwrite SDK into your codebase {% #prompt-sdk %}

If your app already calls Appwrite, skip to [prompt 3](#prompt-table). Otherwise, get a prompt with your real endpoint and project ID from the Console, so the agent doesn't have to guess them:

1. In your project in the Console, open **Apps** > **Add app**.
2. Choose your platform and framework, and click **Continue**.
3. For a web app, keep `localhost` as the hostname, then click **Register and continue**.
4. Under **Set up with AI**, click **Copy prompt**, and paste it into your agent.

In an existing app, the agent installs the SDK, adds a client module, and calls `client.ping()` when the app starts. In an empty folder, it clones a starter with a demo control that sends the ping.

Keep the Console wizard open and run your app. You'll know it worked when the **Connection** card shows **Connected - your SDK reached this project.**

{% only_dark %}
![Add app wizard in the Appwrite Console with the Copy prompt button under Set up with AI and the Connection card showing that the SDK reached the project](/images/blog/connect-ai-coding-assistant-to-appwrite/dark/add-app-ai-prompt.avif)
{% /only_dark %}
{% only_light %}
![Add app wizard in the Appwrite Console with the Copy prompt button under Set up with AI and the Connection card showing that the SDK reached the project](/images/blog/connect-ai-coding-assistant-to-appwrite/add-app-ai-prompt.avif)
{% /only_light %}

The wizard only reacts to a project's first ping. If your project received one before, check that `client.ping()` returns `Pong!` in your app instead.

If the browser console shows a CORS error, the hostname you registered doesn't match where your app runs. Before you deploy, add your production domain the same way.

Then ask the agent to save the project ID and endpoint in your repository's agent instructions file (`AGENTS.md`, or `CLAUDE.md` for Claude Code), so the next session starts from them.

## Prompt 3: Create a table for one feature {% #prompt-table %}

```text
I need to store <tasks> for signed-in users, with <a title, a done flag, and a due date>. Propose a TablesDB database, a table, and columns with their types, and say which columns are required. Wait for my OK, then create them in Appwrite.
```

After you approve the writes, open **Databases** in the Console, open the new table, and check the column types on its **Columns** tab. The skills tell the agent to use explicit text types such as `varchar` and `text` instead of the deprecated `string` type.

{% only_dark %}
![Columns tab of a Tasks table in the Appwrite Console, with title as varchar, done as boolean, and dueDate as datetime](/images/blog/connect-ai-coding-assistant-to-appwrite/dark/table-columns.avif)
{% /only_dark %}
{% only_light %}
![Columns tab of a Tasks table in the Appwrite Console, with title as varchar, done as boolean, and dueDate as datetime](/images/blog/connect-ai-coding-assistant-to-appwrite/table-columns.avif)
{% /only_light %}

You can't change a column's type after creation, so if you see a `string` column, ask the agent to delete and recreate it while the table is still empty.

## Prompt 4: Review the permissions the agent set {% #prompt-permissions %}

This is the check that matters most. A table created without explicit permissions has none, so nobody can reach it from your app. Granting `Role.any()` to clear the errors that follow also grants access to guests.

```text
Show me the table-level permissions and the row security setting on the <tasks> table. I want any signed-in user to be able to create a row, and only the row's creator to read, update, or delete it. Change the table to match and list exactly what you changed.
```

That target is the [private rows pattern](/docs/advanced/security/permissions#example-3-private-rows), with row security on and **Create** for all users as the only table-level permission. Open the table's **Security** tab in the Console and check two cards:

- **Permissions** should list **All users** with only **Create** selected. **Read** for **All users** at this level lets every signed-in user read every row, and any permission for **Any** includes guests.
- **Row level security (RLS)** should be on. With it off, Appwrite ignores row permissions, so the per-user permissions from prompt 5 would do nothing.

{% only_dark %}
![Security tab of the Tasks table in the Appwrite Console, with All users allowed to Create only and Row level security turned on](/images/blog/connect-ai-coding-assistant-to-appwrite/dark/table-security.avif)
{% /only_dark %}
{% only_light %}
![Security tab of the Tasks table in the Appwrite Console, with All users allowed to Create only and Row level security turned on](/images/blog/connect-ai-coding-assistant-to-appwrite/table-security.avif)
{% /only_light %}

The agent's tool calls run as your Appwrite account, not as one of your app's users, so a successful call proves nothing about their access. Prompt 5 is the real test.

## Prompt 5: Write the data code {% #prompt-code %}

```text
Using the table you created and this app's Appwrite SDK, add a form to create <tasks> and a list of the signed-in user's <tasks>. Each row should belong to the signed-in user. If the app has no sign-in yet, add email and password sign-up and sign-in first. Then tell me the command to run the app.
```

This is where Skills show up in your diff. Without them, the agent tends to write the legacy `databases.createDocument()` with positional arguments. The TypeScript skill tells it to use `TablesDB` with object parameters, so a web app's create call looks like this:

```js
import { TablesDB, ID, Permission, Role } from 'appwrite';
import { client } from './appwrite';

const tablesDB = new TablesDB(client);

export function createTask(userId, title) {
  return tablesDB.createRow({
    databaseId: '<DATABASE_ID>',
    tableId: '<TABLE_ID>',
    rowId: ID.unique(),
    data: { title, done: false },
    permissions: [
      Permission.read(Role.user(userId)),
      Permission.update(Role.user(userId)),
      Permission.delete(Role.user(userId)),
    ],
  });
}
```

If the diff adds `Databases`, `createDocument`, or `collectionId` to a codebase that didn't use them, the skill likely didn't load. Start a new session, check that your SDK's skill is installed for this tool, and ask again.

Then test it as one of your app's users, who are separate from your Appwrite account:

1. Run the app, sign up as a test user, and create a task.
2. In the Console, open the table. The new row should appear there.
3. Click the row and open its **Permissions** tab. It should list that user for read, update, and delete.
4. Sign up as a second test user and confirm the first user's task doesn't appear.

{% only_dark %}
![Permissions tab of a row in the Appwrite Console, listing one user with Read, Update, and Delete selected](/images/blog/connect-ai-coding-assistant-to-appwrite/dark/row-permissions.avif)
{% /only_dark %}
{% only_light %}
![Permissions tab of a row in the Appwrite Console, listing one user with Read, Update, and Delete selected](/images/blog/connect-ai-coding-assistant-to-appwrite/row-permissions.avif)
{% /only_light %}

If the test fails, match the symptom:

- **401 `user_unauthorized` on create:** the user isn't signed in, or the table lacks **Create** for **All users**.
- **400 `row_invalid_structure` on create:** the code skips a required column.
- **401 on list:** row security is off.
- **The second user sees the first user's task:** **Read** is set at the table level.

# Create your first Appwrite database and row {% #first-database %}

Your agent now has a scoped connection, current SDK knowledge, and one table whose permissions you've checked. To tell a good schema from a guessed one, learn the parts it built: [Create your first Appwrite database](/blog/post/create-your-first-appwrite-database) covers database types, columns, table permissions, and your first row and query.

